# This program writes the text "Hi!" to the TTY device. The TTY
# is device 2 -- unlike the LCDs, which display numbers, the TTY
# interprets each value as an ASCII character code.

; Select the TTY as our output device.
put 2 dvc

; Each character has a numeric code (called its "ASCII code").
; H = 72, i = 105, ! = 33. We write each code to the TTY.
put 72 gp0
copy gp0 out

put 105 gp0
copy gp0 out

put 33 gp0
copy gp0 out

halt

; Study questions:
; - Look at the "Error Console" pane in the TUI. You should see
;   "Hi!" appear there. The TTY device renders numbers as text!
; - What happens if you write the number 10 to the TTY?
;   (Hint: 10 is the ASCII code for "newline".)
; - Try writing your name using ASCII codes. You can find a
;   full ASCII table by searching the web.
